{"timestamp":"2026-08-27T11:46:36.149Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0430b-19f5-71c1-a228-e01a98a983f8","call_id":"call_0TlxHhf4rb6Y9lbrzu9erymu","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Community | OpenAI Developers (https://developers.openai.com/community)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; I usually give Codex a long \"impossible\" task before I go to bed, just to see how it does overnight. ... > I started a session locally on codex app > had to leave so I handed it off to codex cloud (with 2 attempts) > got back to my computer, compared both attempts, picked the better one, synced it back to my computer > continued to iterate on it locally ... The Codex app feels like the missing IDE layer for the Codex CLI.\n### Angel ❄️\n\n@Angaisb_\n\nGPT-5.3 Codex is actually pretty insane with Three.js This Minecraft clone works smoothly and it didn't take too long to make I also tried Opus 4.6, but for some reason it got stuck\n\nFeb 5, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Angel ❄️\n\n@Angaisb_\n\nGPT-5.3-Codex with image gen skill (NBP) I'm loving this model so much. This is one-shot\n\nVideo\n\nFeb 5, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Flavio Adamo\n\n@flavioAd\n\nCodex app is the most 'non-native' app that somehow feels more native than native apps\n\nImage\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Pallav Agarwal\n\n@pallavmac\n\nOk the new Codex app is incredible. You can build and run an Xcode project from Codex directly so you can vibecode iOS apps without opening Xcode!\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Paul Solt\n\n@PaulSolt\n\nThe Codex app feels like the missing IDE layer for the Codex CLI. One-click to open Xcode, run iOS/macOS apps, review diffs, and manage agents. Hard to go back after this.\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Kristen Anderson\n\nJul 29, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### EstePrime\n\n@EstePrimeWorld\n\nThis month was the first time I paid for an AI subscription other than Copilot, although I had occasionally used OpenCode with API keys in the past. I chose Codex, and honestly, it has made me much more productive. It is also genuinely fun to build new features and MVPs with it.\n\nImage: Codex desktop app showing its project selection screen.\n\nJul 26, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### DHH\n\n@dhh\n\nFor complicated agent work, it's amazing how much GPT5.5 has improved. I found 5.2 to be very far behind Opus. Now using Opus 4.7 after 5.5 feels like a big step backwards. Gotta love this level of competion! Strong comeback for OpenAI.\n\nMay 22, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Aman\n\n@Amank1412\n\nOPENAI INTEGRATED GPT-IMAGE-2 INTO CODEX. Developers can generate and swap game textures in real time using natural language no restart needed. In a 2D zombie tower defense demo, environments update in seconds. Huge boost for indie game art iteration. pic.twitter.com/CcADbOUlLZ\n\nApr 25, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Bindu Reddy\n\n@bindureddy\n\nOur ops team has switched to GPT 5.4 for ops and coding tasks It’s just way better for ops, SRE and cloud configuration issues Likely because it’s just a lot more aware of these knowledge bases\n\nApr 8, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### David Marcus\n\n@davidmarcus\n\nIt's wild that every time you run a Codex code review from Claude Code, it finds critical issues. Not 95% of the times, 100%.\n\nMar 28, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Garry Tan\n\n@garrytan\n\nOK Codex is GOAT at finding bugs and finding plan errors\n\nMar 19, 2026\n\nImage\n\nCost $4.14 and 45 minutes.\n\nMar 5, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Mitchell Hashimoto\n\n@mitchellh\n\nI know this is pretty well established at this point, but Codex 5.3 is a much more effective model than Opus 4.6. I went back and forth on both for a bit, but haven't touched Opus at all now for a full week. First model to get me off of Opus... ever. Good job Codex team.\n\nFeb 25, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Dennis Hannusch\n\n@DennisHannusch\n\nI started daily driving Codex with gpt-5.3-codex this week.. it's reaaally good. I've gotten used to complex workflows and context management, but Codex just does what I ask. I keep expecting quality to drop deep into a session, but it doesn't. @OpenAIDevs ya'll cooked!\n\nFeb 11, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Christopher Ehrlich\n\n@ccccjjjjeeee\n\nIt actually worked! For the past couple of days I've been throwing 5.3-codex at the C codebase for SimCity (1989) to port it to TypeScript. Not reading any code, very little steering. Today I have SimCity running in the browser. I can't believe this new world we live in.\n\nImage\n\nFeb 10, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Guillermo Rauch\n\n@rauchg\n\n🆕 GPT 5.3 Codex (xhigh) achieves 90% on Next.js evals out of the box, \"frame-mogging\" the competition so to speak:\n\nImage\n\nFeb 9, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Flavio Adamo\n\n@flavioAd\n\nCodex app is the most 'non-native' app that somehow feels more native than native apps\n\nImage\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Paul Solt\n\n@PaulSolt\n\nThe Codex app feels like the missing IDE layer for the Codex CLI. One-click to open Xcode, run iOS/macOS apps, review diffs, and manage agents. Hard to go back after this.\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n\nJul 28, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Theo - t3.gg\n\n@theo\n\nMy recommendation: just use Sol.\n\nImage: Comparison graphic showing Sol, Terra, and Luna as celestial bodies.\n\nJul 16, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Omar Shahine\n\n@OmarShahine\n\nCodex is phenomenal at creating Swift iOS apps. I just single shotted an app using goals and got 95% of the way there. Iterating is super fast too. Impressed. Much better than Claude Code.\n\nMay 9, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Bindu Reddy\n\n@bindureddy\n\nOur ops team has switched to GPT 5.4 for ops and coding tasks It’s just way better for ops, SRE and cloud configuration issues Likely because it’s just a lot more aware of these knowledge bases\n\nApr 8, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Garry Tan\n\n@garrytan\n\nOK Codex is GOAT at finding bugs and finding plan errors\n\nMar 19, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### MJ\n\n@mjackson\n\nIf you haven't tried Codex yet, you're missing something BIG.\n\nMar 13, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Derya Unutmaz, MD\n\n@DeryaTR_\n\nMy new Sunday morning routine: 1. Get coffee 2. Check GPT-5.4 projects on the Codex App, continue & start new ones 4. Launch ChatGPT 5.4 Pro for fresh brainstorming sessions 5. Think/learn how to use the 90% of AI capabilities I have yet to explore 6. Drink more coffee\n\nMar 8, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### John Jung\n\n@johnjjung\n\nCodex 5.3 xhigh spark has been phenomenal - you’re able to stay in a state of flow building. The focus and power of hitting problem after problem is incredible.\n\nMar 5, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Theo - t3.gg\n\n@theo\n\nAlmost every verifiable problem I've been sent so far has been solved by 5.3 Codex. Please make sure 5.3 Codex can't solve your problem before you submit it to me. I'm a youtuber not a prompt box.\n\nMar 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Dennis Hannusch\n\n@DennisHannusch\n\nI started daily driving Codex with gpt-5.3-codex this week.. it's reaaally good. I've gotten used to complex workflows and context management, but Codex just does what I ask. I keep expecting quality to drop deep into a session, but it doesn't. @OpenAIDevs ya'll cooked!\n\nFeb 11, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Dan Shipper 📧\n\n@danshipper\n\nBREAKING: At the Super Bowl halftime show, Bad Bunny says he prefers Opus 4.6 for vibe coding but turns to 5.3 Codex for gnarly engineering problems. \"Opus pa'' vibear, Codex pa'' lo heavy\" - he said\n\nFeb 9, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Angel ❄️\n\n@Angaisb_\n\nGPT-5.3-Codex with image gen skill (NBP) I'm loving this model so much. This is one-shot\n\nVideo\n\nFeb 5, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Paul Solt\n\n@PaulSolt\n\nThe Codex app feels like the missing IDE layer for the Codex CLI. One-click to open Xcode, run iOS/macOS apps, review diffs, and manage agents. Hard to go back after this.\n\nFeb 2, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Anthony\n\n@kr0der\n\nCodex v0.91.0 has plan mode, and it's really thorough. this one plan took 27% of its context. if you've used Codex you know that 27% is a lot which means it's thoroughly searching before creating plans - no rushing/taking shortcuts. try it out by adding 'collaboration_modes =…\n### DHH\n\n@dhh\n\nFor complicated agent work, it's amazing how much GPT5.5 has improved. I found 5.2 to be very far behind Opus. Now using Opus 4.7 after 5.5 feels like a big step backwards. Gotta love this level of competion! Strong comeback for OpenAI.\n\nMay 22, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Bindu Reddy\n\n@bindureddy\n\nFinally moved 90% of our hard coding workloads over to Codex For some reason Opus seemed to have gotten less performant over time 🤷‍♀️ It’s also refusing more requests Looking forward to even more capable and efficient Codex\n\nApr 11, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### David Marcus\n\n@davidmarcus\n\nIt's wild that every time you run a Codex code review from Claude Code, it finds critical issues. Not 95% of the times, 100%.\n\nMar 28, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Victor Mota\n\n@vimota\n\nOpenAI got me with the 2x increased usage limits on the Codex desktop app. It's a really nice experience. Super smooth - claude code app is so laggy. And the handoff experience is amazing. > I started a session locally on codex app > had to leave so I handed it off to codex cloud (with 2 attempts) > got back to my computer, compared both attempts, picked the better one, synced it back to my computer > continued to iterate on it locally\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI Developers (https://developers.openai.com/)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Automating repetitive work at OpenAI with Codex How Runme and WebMCP turn recurring engineering tasks into reviewable, reusable workflows.\n\n# OpenAI for developers\n\nDocs and resources to help you build with, for, and on OpenAI.\n\n## GPT-5.6: Lower prices and faster speeds.\n\nSol is now over 20% cheaper, Luna is 80% cheaper, and Terra is 20% cheaper, while Fast mode makes Sol up to 2.5× f